>>> [3/3] six concurrency demos (two real sessions)
Test students: U1=61 (seat 711), U2=63 (seat 712); slot=97; mode=PAPER

========================================================================
PART 1: BUG - check-then-insert with no lock and no UNIQUE guard (scratch table)
========================================================================
student 61 got seat 651
student 63 got seat 651
Rows in demo_seatings:
    (651, 97, 61)
    (651, 97, 63)
RESULT: 2 students, 1 distinct seat(s)  ->  DOUBLE BOOKING (the bug)

========================================================================
PART 2: UNIQUE safety net - same race on the real seatings table (P1 partial UNIQUE index)
========================================================================
student 61: seat 651 OK (committed)
student 63: seat 651 REJECTED [23505] duplicate key value violates unique constraint "uniq_active_seat_per_slot"
RESULT: the database refused the second booking; the loser must retry (no double booking).

========================================================================
PART 3: FIX - pg_advisory_xact_lock inside seat_first_free(): second session waits, then takes the NEXT seat
========================================================================
student 61: got seat 651 after 1.51s
student 63: got seat 652 after 1.22s
Active seatings: [(61, 651), (63, 652)]
RESULT: 2 distinct seats for 2 students - both succeeded, no clash, no error.

========================================================================
PART 4: WRITE SKEW - 'a hall may hold at most N' rule, READ COMMITTED vs SERIALIZABLE
========================================================================
[READ COMMITTED] hall 5 currently has 8 active seatings; rule: at most 9
student 61: read count=8, inserted + COMMIT ok
student 63: read count=8, inserted + COMMIT ok
[READ COMMITTED] active seatings now = 10 (limit 9) -> RULE BROKEN
[SERIALIZABLE] hall 5 currently has 8 active seatings; rule: at most 9
student 61: read count=8, COMMIT FAILED [40001] could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
student 63: read count=8, inserted + COMMIT ok
[SERIALIZABLE] active seatings now = 9 (limit 9) -> rule held
(A failed SERIALIZABLE transaction is simply retried; the retry now sees the full hall and is refused.)

========================================================================
PART 5: ISOLATION - READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ (non-repeatable read)
========================================================================
[READ COMMITTED] session A counts ASSIGNED: before = 600, after session B cancelled one and committed = 599
[REPEATABLE READ] session A counts ASSIGNED: before = 600, after session B cancelled one and committed = 600
RESULT: READ COMMITTED sees the change inside one transaction; REPEATABLE READ keeps its snapshot.

========================================================================
PART 6: DEADLOCK - two sessions update the same two seatings in opposite order, then the fix (fixed order)
========================================================================
Bad order: T1 locks 64832 then 64833; T2 locks 64833 then 64832
T1: locked 64832 then 64833 -> COMMIT ok
T2: ABORTED [40P01] deadlock detected
Fixed order: both sessions lock the lower seating_id (64832) first, then 64833
T1: locked 64832 then 64833 -> COMMIT ok
T2: locked 64832 then 64833 -> COMMIT ok
RESULT: opposite lock order -> PostgreSQL kills one session (40P01). Same order -> no deadlock.

Test data restored. All six parts finished.
